// GENERATED by scripts/build-courses.ts from src/lib/courses/content — edit those files, then run: npm run courses
import type { LessonText } from '../types';
const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"APES starts with how ecosystems work. Energy flows through them, and nutrients cycle.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Species interact through competition, predation, mutualism, commensalism and parasitism. Resource partitioning lets competitors coexist by using resources differently."},{"t":"key","text":"Only about 10% of energy transfers from one trophic level to the next (the 10% rule). The rest is lost as heat, as required by the second law of thermodynamics."},{"t":"p","text":"Primary productivity is the rate producers convert sunlight to chemical energy. Gross primary productivity minus the energy producers use for respiration equals net primary productivity (NPP), the energy available to consumers."},{"t":"p","text":"Biomes are defined by temperature and precipitation: tropical rainforests have the highest NPP; deserts and tundra the lowest."}]},{"say":"Matter cycles through ecosystems. Knowing where it's stored and how it moves is key to environmental problems.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Carbon cycle: photosynthesis removes CO₂; respiration, decomposition and combustion release it. Fossil fuels and ocean sediments are long-term reservoirs."},{"t":"list","items":["Nitrogen cycle: nitrogen fixation (bacteria, lightning) turns N₂ into ammonia; nitrification makes nitrates; assimilation brings it into organisms; ammonification returns it to soil; denitrification returns N₂ to the air.","Phosphorus cycle: no atmospheric component; it moves slowly through rocks, soil and water. It's often a limiting nutrient.","Water (hydrologic) cycle: evaporation, transpiration, condensation, precipitation, infiltration and runoff."]},{"t":"p","text":"Human impacts: burning fossil fuels adds CO₂; fertilizers add nitrogen and phosphorus, causing eutrophication."}]}],[{"say":"Biodiversity makes ecosystems resilient and provides services worth trillions of dollars.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Biodiversity includes genetic, species and ecosystem diversity. Species richness is the number of species; evenness is how equally individuals are distributed among them."},{"t":"key","text":"Ecosystem services are benefits humans get from nature: provisioning (food, timber), regulating (climate, flood control, pollination), supporting (nutrient cycling, soil formation) and cultural (recreation, spiritual value)."},{"t":"p","text":"Island biogeography: larger islands and islands closer to the mainland have more species. This applies to habitat \"islands\" like parks surrounded by development."},{"t":"p","text":"Species have ecological tolerance ranges; generalists tolerate many conditions, while specialists need narrow conditions and are more vulnerable."}]},{"say":"After a disturbance, ecosystems rebuild in a predictable sequence called succession.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Primary succession starts on bare rock with no soil (after a lava flow or glacier). Pioneer species like lichens and mosses break down rock to start soil."},{"t":"p","text":"Secondary succession happens where soil remains after a disturbance (fire, abandoned farmland) and is faster."},{"t":"p","text":"Communities progress from fast-growing r-selected species (many offspring, little care) to slower K-selected species (fewer offspring, more care), toward a mature climax community."},{"t":"p","text":"Indicator species reveal environmental conditions; for example, the presence of certain macroinvertebrates shows healthy stream water."}]}],[{"say":"Populations grow, crash and stabilize. Their strategies and limits shape ecosystems.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"r-selected species reproduce quickly with many offspring and little parental care (insects, weeds). K-selected species have few offspring with more care and long lives (elephants, whales)."},{"t":"p","text":"Survivorship curves: Type I (most survive to old age, humans), Type II (constant death rate, birds), Type III (most die young, fish and plants)."},{"t":"p","text":"Populations grow exponentially with unlimited resources, then logistically level off at the carrying capacity (K). Overshoot beyond K leads to a die-off."},{"t":"p","text":"Density-dependent factors (competition, disease, predation) intensify with crowding; density-independent factors (storms, droughts) don't."}]},{"say":"The human population has grown to over 8 billion. Demographics help us understand where it's headed.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Rule of 70: doubling time ≈ 70 ÷ growth rate (in percent). A population growing at 2% doubles in about 35 years."},{"t":"p","text":"Total fertility rate (TFR) is the average number of children per woman; replacement level is about 2.1. Age-structure diagrams (population pyramids) predict growth: a wide base means rapid growth."},{"t":"p","text":"The demographic transition model: preindustrial (high birth and death rates), transitional (death rates fall, rapid growth), industrial (birth rates fall), postindustrial (low birth and death rates)."},{"t":"p","text":"Factors lowering birth rates include education and empowerment of women, access to family planning, and economic development."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A country grows at 3.5% per year. Find its doubling time.","steps":["70 ÷ 3.5"],"answer":"20 years"}]}],[{"say":"Earth's geology and soils shape where resources and hazards are found.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Plate boundaries create hazards: convergent boundaries form volcanoes and mountains; divergent boundaries form ridges; transform boundaries cause earthquakes. The Pacific \"Ring of Fire\" has many volcanoes and earthquakes."},{"t":"key","text":"Soil has horizons: O (organic), A (topsoil), B (subsoil) and C (weathered parent material). Soil texture (sand, silt, clay) affects water holding: sand drains quickly, clay holds water but drains poorly, loam is best for farming."},{"t":"p","text":"A watershed is all the land that drains into a body of water. Human activities anywhere in a watershed (farming, paving, deforestation) affect water quality downstream."}]},{"say":"The atmosphere and global circulation patterns distribute heat and shape climates worldwide.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The atmosphere's layers: troposphere (weather, most air mass), stratosphere (ozone layer absorbs UV), mesosphere, thermosphere."},{"t":"key","text":"Uneven solar heating (more direct at the equator) drives global air circulation in convection cells (Hadley cells). Rising air at the equator brings rain (rainforests); sinking air near 30° latitude brings dry conditions (deserts)."},{"t":"p","text":"The Coriolis effect deflects winds, creating trade winds and westerlies. Rain shadows form when mountains force air up (rain on the windward side) and dry air sinks on the leeward side."},{"t":"p","text":"El Niño (ENSO) weakens trade winds, warming the eastern Pacific and shifting weather patterns worldwide."}]}],[{"say":"Feeding 8 billion people takes enormous amounts of land and water. Farming methods matter for the environment.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Green Revolution used high-yield crops, fertilizers, pesticides and irrigation to greatly increase food production, but with environmental costs."},{"t":"key","text":"Industrial agriculture problems: soil erosion, loss of soil fertility, water depletion, eutrophication from fertilizer runoff, pesticide resistance and loss of biodiversity from monocultures."},{"t":"p","text":"Irrigation methods: flood and furrow irrigation waste a lot of water; drip irrigation is the most efficient. Overirrigation can cause waterlogging and soil salinization."},{"t":"p","text":"Sustainable practices: crop rotation, contour plowing, terracing, no-till farming, windbreaks, cover crops and integrated pest management (IPM, combining biological, physical and limited chemical controls)."}]},{"say":"How we use land for timber, minerals and cities shapes habitats, water and climate.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Clear-cutting removes all trees, causing erosion, habitat loss and loss of carbon storage. Selective cutting and sustainable forestry remove fewer trees and protect ecosystems."},{"t":"p","text":"Mining extracts minerals and fuels. Surface mining (strip mining, mountaintop removal) destroys habitats and can cause acid mine drainage; land reclamation tries to restore mined areas."},{"t":"p","text":"Urbanization increases impervious surfaces (roads, roofs), leading to more runoff, flooding and the urban heat island effect. Urban sprawl increases car dependence. Smart growth uses mixed-use, walkable, transit-friendly design."},{"t":"p","text":"Ecological footprint measures the land and water needed to support a person's lifestyle."}]}],[{"say":"Most of the world's energy still comes from nonrenewable sources. Each has trade-offs.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Fossil fuels (coal, oil, natural gas) supply most global energy. Burning them releases CO₂ and pollutants. Coal is the most polluting; natural gas burns cleaner but methane leaks are a potent greenhouse gas."},{"t":"p","text":"Electricity from fossil fuels: burning fuel heats water into steam, which spins a turbine connected to a generator. Overall efficiency is often only about 33%."},{"t":"p","text":"Fracking (hydraulic fracturing) extracts natural gas from shale but can contaminate groundwater and trigger small earthquakes."},{"t":"p","text":"Nuclear power uses fission of uranium-235. It produces no CO₂ during operation but creates radioactive waste with long half-lives and carries a small risk of accidents (Chernobyl, Fukushima)."}]},{"say":"Renewable energy and conservation are key to a sustainable future. Each source has strengths and limits.","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Solar: photovoltaic cells convert sunlight to electricity; no emissions but intermittent.","Wind: turbines are cheap and clean but intermittent and can affect birds and bats.","Hydroelectric: dams provide steady power but flood habitats and block fish migration.","Geothermal: uses Earth's internal heat; reliable but location-limited.","Biomass: burning wood or making biofuels; renewable but can release CO₂ and require large areas of land.","Hydrogen fuel cells: produce only water but need energy to make hydrogen."]},{"t":"key","text":"Energy conservation and efficiency (insulation, LED lights, efficient vehicles, smart grids) are often the cheapest way to reduce environmental impact."}]}],[{"say":"Air pollution harms human health and ecosystems. Knowing the major pollutants helps us fight it.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Primary pollutants are emitted directly (CO, NOₓ, SO₂, particulates). Secondary pollutants form in the atmosphere (ozone and smog)."},{"t":"p","text":"Photochemical smog forms when NOₓ and volatile organic compounds react with sunlight to make ground-level ozone, a respiratory irritant. It peaks on sunny afternoons in cities with heavy traffic."},{"t":"p","text":"Thermal inversions trap cool, polluted air under a layer of warm air, worsening smog."},{"t":"p","text":"The Clean Air Act regulates major pollutants. Catalytic converters reduce car emissions; scrubbers reduce power plant emissions."}]},{"say":"Some air pollution falls back to Earth as acid rain, and some of the worst pollution is indoors.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Acid deposition forms when SO₂ and NOₓ react with water to make sulfuric and nitric acids. It lowers the pH of lakes and soils, harms aquatic life, leaches nutrients and damages buildings."},{"t":"p","text":"Limestone (calcium carbonate) can neutralize acid; areas with granite bedrock are more vulnerable."},{"t":"p","text":"Indoor air pollutants include radon (a radioactive gas from rocks, a leading cause of lung cancer), carbon monoxide from faulty heaters, asbestos, mold, and smoke from burning wood or dung for cooking (a major problem in developing countries)."},{"t":"p","text":"Noise pollution disrupts animal communication and causes stress in people."}]}],[{"say":"Clean water is essential for life, but many human activities pollute it.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Point sources come from one location (factory pipes, sewage plants); nonpoint sources come from many places (farm runoff, urban stormwater) and are harder to regulate."},{"t":"p","text":"Eutrophication: excess nitrogen and phosphorus cause algal blooms. When algae die, decomposers use up oxygen, creating hypoxic dead zones (like in the Gulf of Mexico)."},{"t":"p","text":"Other pollutants: oil spills, plastics (microplastics), heavy metals like mercury, and thermal pollution (warm water from power plants holds less oxygen)."},{"t":"p","text":"Sewage treatment: primary (physical removal of solids), secondary (bacteria break down organic matter), and sometimes tertiary (remove nutrients and chemicals)."}]},{"say":"Waste and toxic chemicals can harm both ecosystems and people. Reducing them protects everyone.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Bioaccumulation is the buildup of a toxin in an organism over its life; biomagnification is the increase in concentration at higher trophic levels. Persistent toxins like mercury, DDT and PCBs biomagnify."},{"t":"p","text":"Solid waste goes to sanitary landfills (lined, with leachate collection and methane capture), incinerators (reduce volume but can release pollutants), or is recycled and composted. The waste hierarchy: reduce, reuse, recycle."},{"t":"p","text":"Dose-response: LD50 is the dose that kills 50% of a test population. Toxicity depends on dose, and some chemicals are endocrine disruptors."},{"t":"p","text":"Infectious diseases (malaria, cholera, tuberculosis) are linked to environmental conditions like poor sanitation and climate."}]}],[{"say":"Two global atmospheric problems: a thinning ozone layer and a warming climate. One is being solved; the other is ongoing.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Stratospheric ozone absorbs harmful UV radiation. CFCs (from old refrigerants and aerosols) release chlorine that destroys ozone. The Montreal Protocol (1987) phased out CFCs, and the ozone layer is slowly recovering."},{"t":"p","text":"The greenhouse effect is natural, but human emissions of CO₂ (fossil fuels, deforestation), methane (agriculture, landfills) and nitrous oxide (fertilizers) enhance it, raising global temperatures."},{"t":"p","text":"Effects of climate change: rising sea levels (thermal expansion and melting ice), more extreme weather, shifting species ranges, melting permafrost (releasing more methane), and ocean acidification as oceans absorb CO₂, harming coral and shellfish."}]},{"say":"Global trade spreads invasive species, while habitat loss and other pressures push many species toward extinction.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Invasive species are non-native species that spread rapidly, often because they lack natural predators. Examples include zebra mussels, kudzu, cane toads and Asian carp. They outcompete native species and disrupt ecosystems."},{"t":"p","text":"The biggest threats to biodiversity (HIPPCO): Habitat destruction, Invasive species, Pollution, Population growth, Climate change and Overexploitation."},{"t":"p","text":"Protection strategies:"},{"t":"list","items":["Laws: the Endangered Species Act (US) and CITES (international wildlife trade).","Protected areas and wildlife corridors connecting fragmented habitats.","Captive breeding and habitat restoration."]}]}]];
export default text;
